\section{Method}\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Problem Formulation}\label{sec:formulation}

Given model $\mathcal{M}$ and input $x$, we sample $N$ outputs $\{y_1, \ldots, y_N\}$ at temperature $T > 0$ via constrained decoding \cite{willard2023guided}, where each $y_i$ is an entity set.
A \textbf{per-sample signal} $c_{\text{ps}}: (x, y_i) \to \mathbb{R}$ scores each output independently; an \textbf{instance-level signal} $c_{\text{il}}: (x, \{y_i\}_{i=1}^N) \to \mathbb{R}$ produces a single score per input (notation in Appendix~\ref{app:notation}).
This distinction is consequential for selection: in our study, 4 of 5 signals are instance-level aggregates that assign identical QE scores to all $N$ candidates. Consensus-proximity (MBR) converts these to per-sample rankings (\S\ref{sec:selection_gap}), but these rankings do not improve selection; only natively per-sample signals can, in principle, exploit oracle headroom.

\subsection{Confidence Signals}\label{sec:signals}

Four are \textit{consistency signals}: \textit{structural} (Soft Jaccard [SJ], entity voting confidence [VC]) and \textit{surface} (exact match [EM], Fleiss' $\kappa$ [FK]); log-probability [LP] is the sole \textit{model-internal}, per-sample signal.

\paragraph{Log-probability (model-internal, per-sample).}
For sample $y_i$ with tokens $t_1, \ldots, t_{|y_i|}$:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:logprob}
c_{\text{lp}}(y_i) = \frac{1}{|y_i|} \sum_{j=1}^{|y_i|} \log p_{\mathcal{M}}(t_j \mid t_{<j}, x)
\end{equation}

\paragraph{Exact match voting (surface, instance-level).}
The proportion of samples producing the most common output:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:em}
c_{\text{em}}(x) = \frac{1}{N} \max_{y} \big|\{i : y_i = y\}\big|
\end{equation}

\paragraph{Fleiss' $\kappa$ (surface, instance-level).}
We adapt \citet{fleiss1971agreement} to entity sets: items are unique (text, type) tuples across all $N$ samples; each sample assigns binary present/absent labels, yielding a $|E_\cup| \times 2$ rating matrix:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:fk}
\kappa = \frac{\bar{P} - \bar{P}_e}{1 - \bar{P}_e}
\end{equation}
where $\bar{P}$ is mean observed and $\bar{P}_e$ expected agreement.
Temperature-based sampling introduces mild negative dependence ($-$0.4\% to $-$4.0\%; Appendix~\ref{app:fk_independence}), which does not inflate FK.
Unlike structural signals, FK relies on exact string matching.

\paragraph{Soft Jaccard (structural, instance-level).}
For samples $y_i, y_j$, we group extractions by type, compute max-weight bipartite matching (Hungarian algorithm, token-level IoU), and define:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:sj}
\text{SJ}(y_i, y_j) = \frac{\sum_{m \in M} \text{sim}(a_m, b_m)}{\sum_{g} \max(|g_i|, |g_j|)}
\end{equation}
where $M$ is the matched set, $\text{sim}(a_m, b_m)$ is the token-level IoU between matched spans, and $\max(|g_i|, |g_j|)$ penalizes length asymmetry.\footnote{The denominator uses $\max(|g_i|, |g_j|)$ per type group rather than $|A \cup B|$, penalizing cardinality mismatches more heavily than standard Jaccard; robustness to this choice is verified in Table~\ref{tab:sj_normalizer}.}
The instance-level signal averages over all pairs:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:sj_instance}
c_{\text{sj}}(x) = \frac{1}{\binom{N}{2}} \sum_{i<j} \text{SJ}(y_i, y_j)
\end{equation}

\paragraph{Entity voting confidence (structural, instance-level).}
For each entity $e$ in the union $E_\cup$ of all samples, we compute frequency $\text{freq}(e) = |\{i : e \in y_i\}| / N$.
The instance-level confidence is the mean frequency:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:voting}
c_{\text{vote}}(x) = \frac{1}{|E_\cup|} \sum_{e \in E_\cup} \text{freq}(e)
\end{equation}
When $E_\cup = \emptyset$, we set $c_{\mathrm{vc}}(x) = 0$.\footnote{Equation~\ref{eq:voting} defines the continuous QE signal; for voted-output \textit{selection} (Table~\ref{tab:selection}), we include only entities with frequency ${>}N/2$, following standard majority voting.}

\subsection{Evaluation Framework}\label{sec:evaluation}

\paragraph{Quality estimation.}
Spearman $\rho$ between the signal and instance-level F1, and AUROC classifying instances as above/below median F1.
Both quality estimation and selection exclude instances with no gold annotations: $n{=}529$ on SciERC (from 551), $n{=}2{,}756$ on CoNLL (from 3{,}453).\footnote{On CoNLL, full-set ($n{=}3{,}453$) Spearman $\rho$ values are higher than gold-filtered ($n{=}2{,}756$) by $+0.03$--$0.05$ due to the zero/non-zero boundary inflating correlations, paralleling the effect documented for WNUT-17 in Appendix~\ref{app:additional}.}

\paragraph{Sample selection.}
Best-of-$N$ F1: per-sample signals (LP) directly rank candidates; instance-level signals select the sample closest to consensus.
Baselines: \textit{oracle} (highest-F1 sample) and \textit{single-sample} (one $T{=}1.0$ draw, not argmax decoding; labeled ``Single'' in tables).

\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:setup}

\paragraph{Datasets.}
We evaluate on three English NER benchmarks spanning a difficulty gradient, each serving a distinct role.
\textbf{SciERC} \cite{luan2018scierc} (551 test instances, 6 entity types) is the primary medium-difficulty test bed where models achieve ${\approx}$65\% F1.
\textbf{CoNLL-2003} \cite{tjongkimsang2003conll} (3{,}453 test sentences, 4 entity types, ${\approx}$91\% F1) serves as a ceiling-effect control where limited oracle headroom constrains selection gains.
\textbf{WNUT-17} \cite{derczynski2017wnut} (1{,}287 social media instances, 52\% zero F1) probes the signal-collapse boundary where model capability is insufficient.

\paragraph{Models and sampling.}
Both \textbf{Qwen3-8B} and \textbf{LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct} are fine-tuned with LoRA (rank$=$32, $\alpha{=}64$) for 5 epochs using LLaMA Factory \cite{zheng2024llamafactory} under identical training procedures.
Qwen3-8B's SciERC checkpoint has a minor train--test mismatch (empty thinking tokens); a quantitative analysis rules out this confound (Appendix~\ref{app:thinking_token}).
We draw $N{=}8$ samples at $T{=}1.0$ (primary), with $N{=}16$ for scaling experiments.\footnote{$N{=}16$ with 3 seeds verifies scaling and ranking stability ($\sigma{\leq}0.026$; Table~\ref{tab:multiseed}); gains beyond $N{=}8$ are sublinear (Appendix~\ref{app:n_scaling}).}
Inference uses vLLM with constrained decoding \cite{willard2023guided}.\footnote{Quality estimation uses a single primary seed (seed${=}42$); selection uses 2-seed averages for variance estimation. Three-seed experiments ($\sigma{\leq}0.026$; Table~\ref{tab:multiseed}) confirm ranking stability; the reduced seed count for primary results reflects computational budget.}

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Quality Estimation Results}
\label{sec:qe_results}

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{NER quality estimation ($\rho$ and AUROC; $N{=}8$, primary seed${=}42$; 3-seed stability $\sigma{\leq}0.026$ in Table~\ref{tab:multiseed}). Best in \textbf{bold}. RE results in Appendix~\ref{app:re_extension}. $N{=}16$ in Table~\ref{tab:robustness}.}
\label{tab:quality_estimation}
\begin{tabular}{ll cc cc cc}
\toprule
& & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{SciERC}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{CoNLL (Qwen)}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{CoNLL (LLaMA)}} \\
\cmidrule(lr){3-4} \cmidrule(lr){5-6} \cmidrule(lr){7-8}
Signal & Family & $\rho$ & AUC & $\rho$ & AUC & $\rho$ & AUC \\
\midrule
VC & Structural & \textbf{.379} & \textbf{.663} & .424 & .789 & .426 & .709 \\
SJ  & Structural & .360 & .650 & .436 & .801 & \textbf{.431} & .704 \\
FK & Surface & .267 & .602 & .400 & .774 & .428 & .703 \\
EM  & Surface    & .295 & .614 & \textbf{.451} & \textbf{.816} & \textbf{.431} & .705 \\
\midrule
LP     & Model      & .205 & .578 & .316 & .753 & .307 & \textbf{.761} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

Table~\ref{tab:quality_estimation} presents $\rho$ and AUROC for five signals across tasks and models.\footnote{$\varepsilon{=}0.05$ applies only to selection (\S\ref{sec:selection_gap}); quality estimation is $\varepsilon$-free. Selection F1 is stable across $\varepsilon \in [0, 0.2]$ (Appendix~\ref{app:epsilon_robustness}).}

\paragraph{Signal rankings are difficulty-dependent.}
On SciERC (medium difficulty), structural signals lead (VC $\rho{=}0.379$, SJ $0.360$) over surface ($\rho{\leq}0.295$) and LP ($0.205$); bootstrap CIs confirm the SJ--LP gap ($\Delta\rho{=}{+}0.155$; Appendix~\ref{app:bootstrap_signal}).
On CoNLL (ceiling-effect control, single-sample F1${\approx}$0.91), EM leads both $\rho$ and AUROC ($0.816$); the $\rho$--AUROC dissociation across datasets reflects use-case alignment.
At the signal-collapse boundary---WNUT-17 (52\% zero F1)---all conditional signals collapse (Table~\ref{tab:wnut}); restricting to non-zero instances reshuffles rankings, with EM strongest ($\rho{=}0.360$; Figure~\ref{fig:signal_heatmap}, Appendix~\ref{app:additional}).

%% ============================================================
\subsection{The Correlation-Selection Gap}
\label{sec:selection_gap}

We define the correlation-selection gap as the disparity between a signal's instance-level rank correlation with F1 and its ability to select the best output from $N$ candidates.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Best-of-$N$ NER selection F1 on SciERC ($N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$, 2-seed mean${\pm}\sigma$, Fisher combined $p$ vs.\ single-sample). LP is per-sample; others use consensus-proximity. Best non-oracle in \textbf{bold}. $^\ddagger$Single: one $T{=}1.0$ draw (expected performance of a single sample); Random: uniform random selection from $N$ candidates; $^\dagger$Greedy: deterministic argmax decoding ($T{=}0$), no sampling variance. Non-tied LP subset analysis in \S\ref{sec:discussion}; RE selection in Appendix~\ref{app:re_extension}.}
\label{tab:selection}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
Method & NER F1 & $p$ \\
\midrule
Oracle & $0.778 \pm 0.003$ & --- \\
Greedy ($T{=}0$)$^\dagger$ & $0.663$ & --- \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{3}{l}{\textit{Per-sample signal}} \\
LP           & $\mathbf{0.647} \pm 0.005$ & .29 \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{3}{l}{\textit{Instance-level signals (consensus-proximity selection)}} \\
SJ           & $0.646 \pm 0.002$ & .68 \\
FK           & $0.646 \pm 0.001$ & .79 \\
EM           & $0.637 \pm 0.001$ & .45 \\
VC           & $0.623 \pm 0.004$ & .26 \\
\midrule
Single$^\ddagger$ & $0.645 \pm 0.001$ & --- \\
Random           & $0.610 \pm 0.003$ & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Despite moderate quality estimation, confidence signals yield limited selection gains (Table~\ref{tab:selection}, Figure~\ref{fig:selection_gap}).
Instance-level signals (SJ, FK, EM, VC) assign identical QE scores to all candidates per input; consensus-proximity (MBR) converts these to per-sample rankings (Table~\ref{tab:selection}: SJ 0.646, FK 0.646, EM 0.637, VC 0.623), but none improves over single-sample.
The key question is whether LP---the only natively per-sample signal---can exploit the oracle headroom.

\paragraph{No significant improvement over single-sample.}
On SciERC NER (2-seed sign tests\footnote{Multi-seed NER experiments confirm seed variation is small ($\sigma{\leq}0.026$; Table~\ref{tab:multiseed}).}, $n{=}529$), the oracle (0.778) reveals 13.3~pp of headroom above single-sample (0.645), yet LP achieves only 0.647 ($\Delta{=}{+}0.2$~pp, $p{=}0.29$); no signal consistently improves selection ($p{>}0.05$; TOST equivalence holds under all external anchors for LLaMA-CoNLL but varies by configuration, Appendix~\ref{app:tost}).
The pattern holds across CoNLL and WNUT-17 (Table~\ref{tab:cross_selection}); RE selection results are consistent (Appendix~\ref{app:re_extension}).
CoNLL's near-ceiling single-sample F1 (0.91) with only ${\sim}$4~pp oracle headroom functions primarily as a ceiling-effect control.
Under stringent external TOST anchors ($\delta_0{=}1.0$~pp), some configurations (e.g., Qwen CoNLL) show statistically significant degradation, indicating signals can actively harm selection (Appendix~\ref{app:tost}).
Greedy decoding ($T{=}0$; single forward pass, no sampling overhead) achieves F1${=}0.663$, surpassing all $N{=}8$ selection strategies (best: LP at 0.647).
Instance-level signals predict \textit{which inputs} the model handles reliably, not \textit{which output} is best; closing this gap likely requires trained verifiers \cite{cobbe2021verifiers}.\footnote{For negative results (all $p{>}0.05$), uncorrected tests suffice; the sole positive finding (non-tied LP subset, $+2.9$~pp, $p{=}0.004$; \S\ref{sec:discussion}) survives Bonferroni correction over 5 comparisons.}

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Robustness}
\label{sec:robustness_main}

Consistency signals are robust across $T{\in}\{0.7, 1.0\}$ with sublinear $N$-scaling gains (Table~\ref{tab:robustness}); LP shows the largest temperature sensitivity ($\Delta\rho{=}{+}0.044$).
Signal rankings are stable across seeds (Kendall's $W{=}1.0$ at $N{=}8$; Table~\ref{tab:ranking-stability}).
NER calibration is reasonable (ECE 0.069--0.096; Appendix~\ref{app:calibration}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Scale ablation: Qwen3-8B vs.\ 4B NER quality estimation ($\rho$; $N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$, seed${=}42$). 8B values identical to Table~\ref{tab:quality_estimation}; 4B uses matched evaluation protocol. RE scale ablation in Appendix~\ref{app:re_extension}.}
\label{tab:scale_ablation}
\begin{tabular}{@{}ll cc r@{}}
\toprule
& & \textbf{8B} & \textbf{4B} & $\Delta$ \\
\midrule
CoNLL & SJ & .436 & .422 & $-$.014 \\
      & FK & .400 & .418 & $+$.018 \\
SciERC & SJ & .360 & .422 & $+$.062 \\
       & FK & .267 & .326 & $+$.059 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Scale robustness.}
Repeating with Qwen3-4B (Table~\ref{tab:scale_ablation}), quality estimation is comparable or improved (mean $|\Delta\rho|{=}0.042$), yet selection remains below single-sample on SciERC ($\Delta$ up to $-3.4$~pp) despite 13.0~pp of oracle headroom, and indistinguishable on CoNLL (${<}0.3$~pp, 1.7~pp headroom). Within the 3B--8B range tested across two model families, task difficulty is a stronger predictor of the gap than model scale.
Instance degeneracy also scales with model capability: 8B shows twice the degeneracy of 4B on SciERC (21\% vs.\ 10.4\%) despite comparable within-instance utility correlation (Table~\ref{tab:degeneracy}).
